# CODEOWNERS — who reviews what.
#
# ## Why this file exists
#
# `docs/envoymesh-refactoring-plan.md` §2.4 found that `apps/node/src` had "no
# declared boundary, no declared owner, and no declared public surface". The
# boundary and the surface are now declared and enforced (the manifest and
# `scripts/check-module-boundary.mjs`). **Ownership was the remaining gap** —
# recorded as decision **E10**, which could not be closed by a guess: an
# invented owner map is worse than none, because it looks authoritative.
#
# ## What this is
#
# A deliberately minimal starting point, derived from facts rather than
# preference:
#
#   * the repository remote is `github.com/allenpeng0705/EnvoyMesh`, so the
#     owning handle is `@allenpeng0705`;
#   * 1,244 commits are by that author, with no competing maintainer.
#
# One owner for everything is honest and immediately useful (GitHub will request
# that reviewer). It is **not** a claim about how the code should be divided.
#
# ## The areas a future split would use
#
# Recorded now so the split is cheap when a trigger fires (see the decision
# below). These are the areas the refactoring plan already distinguishes:
#
#   /packages/protocol/     protocol schemas — the reusable leaf everything fans in through
#   /packages/identity/     keys, device certificates, mandates
#   /packages/network/      libp2p wrapper: transports, mDNS, DHT, circuit relay
#   /packages/api/          the RPC contract (see E9 — splitting into CoreNodeService)
#   /apps/node/src/         the node runtime — 483 modules in the manifest, the largest surface
#   /packages/host-connect/ the reusable host/connect layer
#   /packages/node-core/    the shared core (ports, home-fs, slash descriptors)
#   /packages/harness/      ext-agent adapters + Pi runtime
#   /apps/social/           the desktop Social product
#   /apps/envoygo/          the mobile product
#   /apps/relay/            the relay binary — stays lean by design
#   /packages/envoy-*-dart/ the reusable Dart SDKs (reusable/social library split, 42 modules)
#   /scripts/               the boundary checks themselves
#   /.github/workflows/     CI
#
# When splitting, keep `scripts/` and `.github/workflows/` owned deliberately:
# those files *enforce* the boundary, so a change there deserves the same review
# weight as a change to the boundary itself.

# ## Decision — E10, Option 1: **defer the per-area split** (2026-09-12)
#
# This file stays a single catch-all on purpose, and that is now a recorded
# decision rather than an unfinished item.
#
# **Why not per-area lines today.** There is one human committer (1,244 commits,
# `@allenpeng0705`). Twelve area lines would all name the same reviewer: the shape
# of an ownership map without its content, which reads as authoritative and says
# nothing. Deferring costs nothing operationally — GitHub requests that reviewer
# either way.
#
# **What reopens it** (any one of these):
#
#   1. **A second maintainer owns a subsystem** — the split only means something
#      once the names differ.
#   2. **One subsystem's change rate justifies a dedicated reviewer** — measure it
#      (`git log --name-only --since=3.months`), do not go by feel.
#   3. **A second product ships from this tree** (EnvoyCoder / EnvoyAgent) — the
#      reusable/product split already implies areas, and the product-bound side
#      becomes a natural review boundary.
#
# **What to do first when a trigger fires:** build the area map (Option 2 in
# `docs/envoymesh-refactoring-plan.md` §8.8 — an `areas` table in
# `scripts/classify-modules.mjs`, gated by the same completeness rule as the
# classification), then **generate** these lines from it. Do not hand-write them:
# a hand-kept copy drifts the moment a directory moves, which is the same lesson
# as the module-size allowlist and the six-places package list.
#
# Three sub-decisions to settle at that point: (a) granularity — top-level
# directory versus subsystem; (b) owners as GitHub **teams** (needs the repo to
# live in an organisation) versus individuals; (c) whether `scripts/` and
# `.github/workflows/` get their **own** owner, since those files *enforce* the
# boundary and a change there deserves boundary-level review weight.

# Catch-all — see the decision above before replacing it (E10, Option 1).
